= confirmation =
====== short ======
What is a scientific theory

====== related to ======
[[confirmation_holism]]
[[degrees_of_belief]]

[[glymour1980-theoryAndEvidence]]+++

= main =
When one conducts an experiment to test a hypothesis. There is a division
between the rules of the theory that are taken to be, at least provisionally,
necessary. ~~(That was a misguided and misleading way of putting it)~~. We, at
least provisionally, take some part of the theory as beyond question. If things
go badly and in attempting to determine whether of hypothesis is true (e.g. the
value of a particular quantity or constant has no solution - i.e. no value will
satisfy the constraints of our theory and our observations) then we may cast
doubt on the theory itself, but if things go well we simply rely on the theory
to compute the truth value(/quantitative value) In the extreme (and paradigmatic
case) they are taken to be nomologically necessary (and hence, at least, true at
all times and places[[#*1]]) but they may simply be invariant between
experiment instances.
====== *1 ======
That this is not so much as assumption of the regularity of nature, as
an attempt to discern whatever it is that is regular/invariant. We do not
optimistically assume that the laws we discover are invariant, but look for
whatever it is that is invariant and call them laws.

It is these invariant, nomologically necessary (which is to say _regarded_ or
_held to be_ nomologically necessary principles) which constitute the relevant
<q>scientific theory</q>. Newton's theory of gravitation, or the theory of
quantum mechanics, or general relativity are theories in this sense. Consisting
purely of principles that are taken to be true at all times and places. In
general this is what is meant by a scientific theory. And when we talk of a
scientific theory being confirmed it is such theories that provide the
paradigmatic examples.

But, of course, there are scientific theories about particulars too.
The big bang theory or the theory that the [[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vinland_Map|Vinland map]] is a forgery, or the
theory that humans first non-seasonal settlements predate the development of
agriculture.

These are at best second rate theories. They are apt to be called hypotheses
rather than theories, or once strongly confirmed called discoveries rather than
theories. They are applications of the invariant theory, plus particular
observations to determine other particulars. In the worst case they may lead to
revision of a proper invariant theory when no hypotheses regarding the further
particulars can explain/fit with the observed evidence.

= ad hoc hypotheses =
:my_thesis:problem:
Ad hoc means "for this" and refers to something created or done for a specific
purpose or problem, often on a temporary basis.

An Ad hoc hypothesis allows one to maintain a theory without modification in
the face of conflicting evidence. So theory T predicts that we will observe O.
We don't observe O but we don't change our confidence in T or
our expectations of the future observations it predicts.

Now we might have an explanation of why the predictions didn't come off in this
case that are not ad hoc. Perhaps the switch was off, or a fuse had burnt out.
But then we can investigate and make observations to independently confirm this
extraneous factor. And we will lose credence in our theory if that factor
doesn't occur in future and the prediction still don't come off. The ad hoc
assumption becomes an enthymeme in hindsight.

= is it confirmation if we dont know it yet? =
[[confirmation_holism]]
:RLN: Suppose that someone hasn't worked out that observation or other
is a deductive consequence of a theory.

We don't even need to engage the machinery of hypothetico-deductivism to
describe such a situation.  Suppose there is some observable thing that,
once it _has been_ worked out, is widely taken as very strong evidence for
believing the theory in question, but was known for some time before the
connection was worked out.

One supposes that something like this must have occurred in the development of
general relativity. Certainly, that general relativity explained (/retrodicted)
the advance in mercury's perehelion was given by Einstein as a reason to believe
general relativity and for many people this may be one of the first things they
are told about general relativity, so that there is no period of time in which
they understand the theory but do not know that it has this consequence.
But, at least for Einstein himself, surely the situation was otherwise. There
must, of course, have been a period in which he _suspected_, but was not
absolutely sure that general relativity explained this phenomena. He must have
been motivated to try to explain Mercury's perehelion, _prior_ to actually
coming up with the explanation. But even before this - surely there was a time
when he was aware of the theory of relativity but had not yet connected it with
Mercury's perehelion.
Surely we have good (though not conclusive) reason for beleiving that there
was a time _after_ Einstein first formulated the theory of general relativity,
but _before_ he considered whether there was a connection between the two.
~~Actually no explaiing the advance perehelion may have been one of his~~
~~initial reasons for developing general relativity in the first place~~
~~but it doesnt matter - the mere possibility is enough, though difficult~~
~~for a modal skeptic to build upon~~

===== key question =====
Does that evidence confirm the theory or not. No one actually has increased
their confidence/credence in the theory. No one has realized the connection
between the evidence and the theory yet. (Even though some people had observed
the relevant phenomena.)

If we say it is confirmed then it seems surely we are using <q>confirmed</q> in
a normative way.  That evidence _ought_ at least once one considers it,
confirm one's belief in the theory. 

In the other direction we say that XYZ observation confirmed belief in a theory.
Say the observation of the deflection of starlight around the Sun in 1919. Until
the observation ws made and it caused people confidence in T to increase.

This is not unusual - a kind of transitivity of predicates. Things that are apt
to cause 

This is sort of connected with Quine's distinction between eternal and occasion
sentences. Is <q>XYZ confirms T</q> false before a time when people first made
the connection and true afterwards. Or is it an eternal sentence, one whose
truth value we did not _know_ before but which was true all along despite our
ignorance.

We don't need to say that one of these usages is correct and the other one wrong.


It seems like the dispositionalist/naturalist has a hard time here, if they want
to claim that 

[[quinean_reasoning]] doesnt seem to help very much
Many presumed competnet speakers of the target language assert XYZ and yet deny
T, so the conditional is not part of what the linguist observes
There is (or at least may be) a chain of obvious truths. A range of
conditionals that all competent spoeakers assent to _when prompted_

Thus if we consider the linguist as adopting a quasi-socratic method,
largely consisting of asking leading questions, then we can see both that the
linguists questions may lead subjects to alter their verdicts, and for that
very reason they may be more of a philosopher than we had realised.

Still, there may not be any such chain. There may be a time when many erstwhile
competent speakers accept both XYZ and not T, even once their attention is
drawn to it. It would be implausibly optimistic to believe that those who
correctly judge the connection between evidence and hypothesis are always in
the majority. Or is history simply written by the victor and whatever theory
does in fact gain the majority of believers viewed as being confirmed by the
evidence that did in fact confirm in hindsight?


== objection: it ought to be categorical ==
If one provides <q>one ought to reaons well if one wants to manifest the
structure of their beliefs in words</q> of <q>if they want to maintain a
unchanging connection between their linguistic expressions and their
non-linguistic models</q> then this is wrong.

One ought to reason well FULL STOP
One does not reason well to achieve this or that objective. And the person who
reasons badly is doing something wrong or incorrect – not merely something that
does not lead to outcomes that they desire.

This is silly - we do not know why we do it, what goal it fulfilled. But this
isn't to say that there is one. We can in hindsight say that it was, in some
sense, assumed that everyone _should_ maintain a consistent mapping between
linguistic and non-linguistic representations, or perhaps that everyone _should_
be able to explain themselves and their motivations (consider the teachers anger
when a student says they don't know why they misbehaved). But this was simply an
ignorant and mistaken theory. There was some benefit in doing so, but one that
(ironically enough) we could not articulate and so we simply said God requires
it, or in some other way it is simply virtuous to do this.

We are disagreeing with the holders of those views. We have a different account
of what reasoning is . But that difference alone does not guarantee that they
are right and we are wrong. We ought not, at least if we are to reason well,
dismiss all non-normative accounts of reasoning simply because the ancients
though reasoning well was an end in itself.

One may as well say, it is an essential part of reasoning that it is mysterious
why we should reason well. Therefore no unmysterious explanation of reasoning
can ever be correct
